window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][72] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> prioritization · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Sales asks you to build a custom report for one large customer who is threatening to leave. Do you build it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, the customer is important&quot; or &quot;No, we never build one-offs,&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sizes the customer's value, checks whether the need is shared, scopes the smallest version, names the cost to the roadmap, and decides by a stated criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the request from the underlying job, tests whether a configurable version serves others, time-boxes the work, names the precedent cost, and says what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would not answer yes or no yet. I would ask what the customer is trying to do with the report, because &quot;a custom report&quot; is one way to meet a need, and the need may be shared by other customers. Then I would size three things. First, the customer's value: say the account is 6% of annual revenue and renews in three months (invented). Second, how many other customers have asked for something similar, from support and sales notes. Third, the cost: two engineers for three weeks (invented), which is work that would otherwise go to the roadmap. My options differ in kind. Build exactly what was asked, gaining the account and giving up three weeks of roadmap plus a precedent that large customers get one-offs. Build a configurable export or report builder that covers this case and others, which costs more now and serves more customers. Offer an interim workaround, such as a data export and a template the customer's analyst can use this week, which is cheap and reversible. I recommend the workaround this week, to remove the immediate threat, and a time-boxed scoping of the configurable version, with the decision based on how many other customers show the same need. The cost is that the customer gets less than they asked for at first, and I would explain why and when they get more. I would change the recommendation if the contract names the report, if the account is large enough that losing it changes the company's plans, or if scoping shows the configurable version costs about the same as the one-off.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Engineering proposes removing a feature that is rarely used but that a few large enterprise customers rely on. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Counts users and removes it because &quot;usage is low,&quot; or keeps it because &quot;customers might leave.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Measures revenue and strategic weight of the affected customers, estimates the carrying cost, considers alternatives, and plans the removal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Classifies the decision as one-way for the customers, reduces the irreversibility with a notice period and a migration path, and states the conditions that would make keeping it right.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Usage counts are the wrong first measure: a feature used by 2% of accounts can be used by 20% of revenue. So I would ask four questions. Which customers use it, and what share of revenue and renewals do they represent (invented example: 5 accounts, 9% of annual revenue)? What does it cost to keep, in engineering time, incident risk and the drag it adds to other changes? What do the customers do with it, and is there an alternative path? And what is the decision's reversibility? For the customers, removal is a one-way door. They have built workflows on it. That points to the mode &quot;decide with evidence and reduce the irreversibility,&quot; not &quot;decide quickly.&quot; My options: keep it as is; keep it but freeze it, with no new investment; replace it with a supported alternative and migrate customers; remove it with a long notice period. Keeping it as is gives up engineering capacity. Removal gives up the revenue risk and the trust of the customers, whose renewals may be tied to the feature. Replacing it asks us to build something, and costs the most effort but is the only option that serves both sides. I would recommend freezing it now, investigating a replacement path with the five customers, and deciding on removal when we know the replacement cost and each customer's migration effort. If the carrying cost turns out to be large and the replacement path is cheap, I would remove it with at least a quarter's notice and a named migration contact. I would change my mind if the customers' contracts name the feature or if the replacement cost exceeds the revenue at risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product thinking · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customer support costs have doubled in a year, while the customer base grew 30%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a chatbot&quot; or &quot;hire more efficient agents,&quot; with no diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes cost into volume and cost per contact, finds the drivers, and addresses the largest.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates fixed facts from conventions, computes the per-customer change, finds root causes upstream of support, and chooses between deflecting contacts and removing the reason for them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would start with arithmetic, because &quot;doubled&quot; and &quot;grew 30%&quot; are not the same thing. If total cost doubled and customers grew 1.3 times, cost per customer rose by about 54% (2 divided by 1.3, which is 1.54). So growth explains part of the increase, but not most of it. Decompose cost. Cost equals contacts times cost per contact. Contacts equal customers times contact rate. Cost per contact equals handling time times labor cost per minute plus tooling. I would find which factor moved: did the contact rate rise, did handling time lengthen, or did the labor cost change? Suppose the data (invented) show the contact rate per customer up 40%, handling time up 10%, and the rest from labor. The contact rate is the largest driver, so I would examine its reasons. Break contacts by reason and by customer cohort: billing questions, delivery issues, product defects, &quot;how do I&quot; questions. Say billing questions are 35% of the increase (invented). The first-principles question is: what is the user trying to do, and why can they not do it without help? If the billing page is confusing, the fix is a clearer page, which removes contacts rather than handling them. The options differ in kind. A chatbot deflects contacts at the support layer, is quick to build, and leaves the root cause. Improving the billing page removes contacts at the source, takes longer, and benefits customers who never contacted support. Training agents reduces handling time and affects the smaller factor. I would recommend working on the top two contact reasons at the source, with a limited deflection tool for the long tail. I would measure contacts per customer by reason, not total cost, with customer satisfaction as the <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> so that cost cuts do not come from worse service. I would change course if the top reasons turn out to be outside our control, such as carrier delays.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product thinking · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your VP asks the team to grow daily active users by 20% this quarter. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run growth campaigns and add notifications,&quot; with no discussion of the target.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what the target is for, decomposes the metric, proposes options and guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Anticipates how the metric will be gamed, separates real value from activity, proposes counter-metrics and staged tests, and negotiates the target with a reason.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would first ask what the target is meant to achieve, because daily active users is a proxy. If the underlying goal is more people getting value, then there are targets that measure it more directly, and I would raise that with the VP rather than silently optimize a proxy. If the VP has a reason for this one, such as a board commitment, I would take it as given and work within it. Decompose the metric. Daily active users equal new users plus returning users, minus those who stop. I would look at what drives each, and where the cheapest honest gain lies: activation of new users, resurrection of lapsed ones, or frequency among those who already use the product. Then I would think through second-order effects, because this target invites gaming. Notifications can raise daily activity by prompting opens that deliver no value, which increases the count and erodes trust and opt-in rates later. That is a classic <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> outcome. So I would pair the target with counter-metrics: notification opt-out rate, sessions with a meaningful action, and 30-day retention. My plan would test two or three levers in order of honesty about value: a better first-week experience, a reminder tied to something the user said they care about, and a content improvement. Each is tested as an <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a> with the counter-metrics watched. I would tell the VP up front what I expect: a gain of a certain size from the honest levers (a range, with low confidence until we have a first read), and what extra gain would come only from tactics I would not recommend. If the full 20% is reachable only by tactics that damage retention, I would propose a smaller target for DAU with a retention floor. I would change my plan if the first experiments show frequency, not activation, is where the headroom is.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a decision you made with incomplete information.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story with no stated options, no stated information gaps, and an outcome that is the whole point.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the options, the missing information, the decision rule and the result, and says what was learned.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows how the candidate sized the cost of being wrong, made the decision reversible, set a trigger to revisit it, and separates decision quality from the outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story, numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for a scheduling feature at a software company. Two weeks before a planned release, a customer-facing team told me that a partner calendar integration might behave differently in time zones than we had tested. We could not reproduce it, and the partner's documentation was unclear. I had three options: delay the release by three weeks to get the partner's confirmation, release with the integration enabled for everyone, or release without the integration and add it later. &quot;I defined the cost of being wrong in each case. A wrong meeting time is more than an annoyance, because someone misses a meeting. That made the integration the risky part, and the rest of the release was fine. A delay would have cost three weeks on a feature that customers had asked for. Releasing the rest without the integration would give up about a third of the expected use but carried no risk of wrong times. &quot;I chose to release without the integration, put the integration in a small beta with 30 volunteer customers (invented), and set a rule: expand when we saw no time-zone errors across 500 scheduled meetings (invented), and pause if one was reported. I told my manager at the time that I was about 60% confident the integration was fine and that this was a judgment, not a measurement. &quot;The beta found one edge case with a half-hour time zone offset. We fixed it and expanded the integration five weeks later. I would make the same decision again. If the problem had not appeared, I would still have given up about five weeks of integration use, which I counted as a known cost. What I changed afterward was to ask partners for sandbox environments early in the project, because the information gap was avoidable.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> We launched a &quot;saved searches&quot; feature and very few people use it. How would you find out why?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists reasons (&quot;people don't know about it, it's not useful&quot;) and says &quot;I'd survey users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies what &quot;few&quot; means, structures along an adoption path, prioritizes and states what each check would show.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates &quot;the need is small&quot; from &quot;the need is large but the feature is not found,&quot; writes rejectable hypotheses, and ties each result to a decision.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> First I'd clarify. What does &quot;few&quot; mean: a share of weekly users, and against what expectation? How long since launch, and on which platforms? The decision matters too: are we deciding whether to invest further, improve how people find it, or retire it? I'll assume that 3% of weekly users have tried it against an expectation of 15% (invented), eight weeks after launch, and that the decision is whether to invest, fix discovery, or retire. I'll structure along an adoption path, because the decision is which stage to fix: do people need this (is the underlying behavior common), do they see the entry point, do they try it, does it work when they try, and do they come back. Need comes first because it decides everything else: if almost nobody repeats searches, no discovery fix will help. I'd check two things first, both cheap. Need: what share of users run the same search three or more times in a month? Say it is 25% (invented). Exposure: what share of those users ever saw the entry point? Say it is 20% (invented). Hypotheses: H1, the need is real and the entry point is not seen. Prediction: among repeat searchers, few have seen the button, and those who did try it at a decent rate. I'd reject H1 if exposure is high and the try rate is still low. H2, the feature is seen but first use fails. Prediction: many starts, few saved searches. H3, the need is narrower than assumed. Prediction: few users repeat searches. With the data I assumed, need is large and exposure is low, so H1 is supported and H3 is not. I'd test moving the entry point to the results page for 20% of users, with saved searches per active user as the metric and search satisfaction as a guardrail. I'd change my view if exposure rises and adoption stays flat, which would point to H2 and a usability problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should a ride-hailing app add a &quot;schedule a ride in advance&quot; feature?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, many apps have it&quot; or a pros and cons list with no recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Builds a decision structure (value, feasibility, viability, risk), evaluates each, and recommends with the cost stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the core feasibility problem (promising availability in the future), proposes a staged test with a defined segment, and states what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is a decision question, so I'll use four branches that cover it: is the need real, can we deliver it, does it work for the business, and what is the risk and can we undo it. Value: who needs to know a ride is arranged? Typically early-morning airport trips and people with fixed appointments. I'd check what share of rides today are requested on the same day for a known time, and how often riders cancel a ride they could not guarantee. Say 8% of rides fall in this group (invented). Feasibility: the hard part is not the screen but the promise. At request time we cannot be sure a driver will be available in three days. Options include guaranteeing a driver by committing capacity, offering a best-effort reservation, or confirming a ride only an hour before. Each trades reliability for cost. Viability: a guaranteed ride costs money when supply is thin, so we need a reservation fee or a cancellation rule, and drivers need a reason to accept scheduled rides. Risk and reversibility: a failed scheduled ride is worse than a failed on-demand ride because a rider counted on it, and the damage to trust persists. But a pilot is reversible. I recommend a pilot for airport and early-morning trips in one city, with a reservation fee, giving up broad coverage to learn whether we can keep the promise. The success metric is the on-time pickup rate for scheduled rides, with driver cancellation rate as the counter-metric. I would extend only if on-time pickup stays above the rate of on-demand rides. I would stop if the fee deters most riders or the promise fails often.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> On a marketplace for handmade goods, the number of listings is growing quickly but sales per listing are falling. Structure the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists possible causes (competition, quality, pricing) without noticing that the metric is a ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes the ratio, separates supply from demand, and prioritizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notices the metric can fall mechanically when listings grow faster than demand, asks whether it is a problem, checks concentration, and links the finding to a decision.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start by noticing that &quot;sales per listing&quot; is a ratio: total sales divided by listings. It can fall because sales fall, because listings grow faster than sales, or both. So the first question is what happened to total sales. If total sales are flat while listings grow 30% (invented), sales per listing fall about 23% mechanically (1 divided by 1.3 is 0.77), and the cause is dilution, not a deterioration. Then I'd ask whether it is a problem at all. For a marketplace, more supply is good if it serves unmet demand and bad if it spreads a fixed number of buyers more thinly and drives good sellers away. The decision is whether to limit or curate supply, improve discovery, or accept the change. I'd structure with supply, demand and matching. Demand: buyers, conversion, repeat buying. Supply: listing quality, price, distribution across sellers. Matching: how well search and recommendations connect buyers to the listings that suit them. Priorities: total sales trend (cheap, decisive), and the distribution of sales across listings (cheap): do the top 10% of listings take most sales, and is that share rising? A rising concentration says new listings are not being discovered or are weaker. Then check the quality of new listings and the match between new listings and search queries. Hypotheses: H1, new listings are weaker and buyers favor established ones; prediction: new listings' conversion is lower and concentration rises. H2, demand is flat and supply growth is dilution; prediction: total sales flat, per-listing sales of old listings unchanged. I would reject H1 if new listings convert as well as old ones. My recommendation depends on the result: if H2, focus on demand growth and avoid punishing sellers; if H1, work on discovery for new listings and on seller guidance.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your CEO says, &quot;Users don't love our product.&quot; How would you turn that into something you can act on?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd do a survey or NPS to see if that's true.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what the CEO observed, defines &quot;love&quot; as measurable behaviors, segments, and frames a question with a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats the statement as a hypothesis about a segment, converts it into several measurable claims, orders the checks, and agrees on the decision with the CEO before analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would treat this as a hypothesis from someone with a signal, and my first job is to learn what the signal was. I'd ask the CEO what prompted it: a number, a review, a conversation, or a competitor comparison. I'd also ask what decision it should drive: a roadmap change, a retention program, a repositioning. Then I'd turn &quot;love&quot; into behaviors we can measure: users return without prompting (retention and frequency), they recommend (referrals, ratings), they would be unhappy to lose it (a standard survey question such as how disappointed they would be), and they pay or upgrade. I'd also ask: which users? &quot;Users&quot; is rarely uniform; the statement may be true for one segment and false for another. So the frame becomes: &quot;Which user segments retain and recommend at lower rates than we expect, and what do they say is missing?&quot; The decision: which segment and which problem the next quarter's roadmap should address. Structure by cohort and segment: new versus returning, by use case, by acquisition source. Prioritize: retention by cohort and segment is already available, so I'd start there. Then qualitative checks on the weakest segment: ten interviews and a read of the support themes. I'd come back to the CEO within a week with a one-page answer: which segments are weaker, how much, and what the weakest segment says. If the data shows retention is healthy, I'd say so, and ask what else prompted the concern, because the signal may be about something I haven't measured, such as word of mouth.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Churn at a B2B product has doubled in two quarters. You have ten minutes. Structure the problem and tell me the first three things you'd check and why.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists causes (price, competitors, product, service) and says &quot;look at customer feedback.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies churn's definition and timing, builds a MECE structure, chooses three checks with reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Checks measurement and renewal timing before causes, uses concentration to decide where to look, orders checks by likelihood and cost, and includes a reversible mitigation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Clarify (two minutes).</em> Is churn counted by customers or by revenue? Is it logos lost or contracts not renewed? Doubling from what, say from 2% to 4% a quarter (invented)? Was it gradual or a step? Is it concentrated in a segment, a plan or a size? Did anything change: price, packaging, support, product? And what is the decision: where to focus a retention effort? <em>Structure.</em> Churn is a ratio too: customers lost divided by customers up for renewal. So I'd ask first whether the numerator or the denominator changed, which means checking renewal timing: a large annual cohort renewing this quarter would raise churn without any change in behavior. Then I'd cut by who left (segment and size) and why (cause type: product value, price, competition, service, customer's own situation). <em>First three checks, with reasons.</em> 1. <strong>Measurement and renewal mix.</strong> Cheap, and it could change the whole story. Compare churn within each renewal cohort rather than in aggregate. If churn within cohorts is flat, the doubling is a timing effect. 2. <strong>Concentration.</strong> Cheap and high impact. Which segment, plan or size explains most of the lost revenue? If three large accounts explain half of it, the response is different from a broad slide. 3. <strong>Recent changes.</strong> The release, pricing and support log for the two quarters. A price rise or a plan change would show as churn concentrated in the affected plan after its date. <em>Hypotheses.</em> H1: the change is a renewal-mix effect; I'd reject it if churn within each cohort also rose. H2: a pricing or packaging change drove churn in one plan; I'd reject it if the plan's churn rate did not change after the date. H3: competitor pressure in one segment; I'd look for churned accounts that moved to the same alternative. <em>Conclude.</em> &quot;I'd report which of these holds with a confidence level. In parallel I'd start a reversible mitigation for at-risk accounts in the affected segment: a customer-success call and an offer review for accounts that are close to renewal. I'd change course if the check shows churn is a mix effect, in which case the mitigation is unnecessary and I would put the effort into confirming the forecast for the next quarter.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time your first diagnosis of a problem was wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story in which the candidate was &quot;mostly right&quot; or the error was somebody else's, with no change in behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the first hypothesis, how it was tested, what showed it was wrong, and what the real cause was.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows what the candidate did to avoid anchoring, how quickly they dropped the hypothesis, what they changed in their working method afterward, and the cost of the error.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story; numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for an online store's mobile checkout. Conversion fell from 3.1% to 2.6% over two weeks. My first hypothesis was that a new payment provider we had added caused failures, because the timing matched the release. I was fairly sure, and I told my manager that I thought it was payments. &quot;I checked it as a hypothesis I could reject. If payments were failing, payment success rates would have fallen. They had not: success was 97% before and after. I dropped the hypothesis the same day, about six hours after I had stated it. &quot;The next check was a cut by traffic source. The drop sat almost entirely in mobile web visitors from a new advertising campaign, which accounted for 30% of traffic in the second week and converted at 0.8% (invented). The overall rate fell because the mix changed, not because checkout worsened. Within each source, conversion was flat. &quot;The cost of my first diagnosis was about half a day of an engineer's time, which I had asked to review payment logs. What I changed afterward was my default order of checks. I now check who the traffic was before I check what the product did, and the first question in our weekly metric review is 'what changed in the audience?' I also write my hypothesis down with what would reject it before I look at data, because I noticed I had been looking for confirmation.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Support tickets have doubled in three months, and the head of support wants to hire more agents. How do you think about it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Agrees to hire, or lists possible causes with no structure and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Splits tickets into users times contact rate, looks at categories, separates growth in users from a higher rate, and compares the options by cost and time to take effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats hiring as a bridge, ties each cause to a cheap check, sizes what fixing the top cause would save in agent time, and says what would make hiring the right call.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would first ask whether the number of users or the rate of tickets per user doubled, because they point to different problems. Tickets are users times the contact rate. Suppose tickets rose from 20,000 to 40,000 a month while active users rose from 500,000 to 550,000 (invented). The contact rate went from 40 to 72.7 tickets per 1,000 users, so almost all of the doubling is a higher rate, not growth. Next, where the tickets come from. By category, suppose billing questions rose from 5,000 to 17,000 and everything else from 15,000 to 23,000 (invented). Billing explains 12,000 of the 20,000 extra tickets, 60%, so it is the first place to look. Then I would ask which cause family fits billing: a product or policy change near the start of the rise (a new pricing page, a billing release), a confusing flow, an incident that left a backlog, a change in how tickets are counted, or a seasonal peak. The cheapest check is the date the billing tickets began to rise against the release and pricing calendar, then a read of 50 billing tickets for the common complaint. Options. Hiring: 20,000 extra tickets at 12 minutes each is 4,000 hours a month; at about 105 productive hours per agent that is 38 agents, and recruiting and training take about two months. Fixing the top cause could remove up to 12,000 tickets, which is 2,400 hours, or 23 agents' worth of work. So I would fix the billing cause, add temporary capacity for the weeks in between, and set a hiring trigger: if the contact rate stays above 50 per 1,000 users after the fix, the rest is real demand and hiring is right. I would change my mind if the rate rose in every category, which points to a product quality problem or a counting change, or if the billing tickets turned out to be unavoidable, such as required notices. Then more agents are the correct answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your team can build a messaging service in-house or buy one from a vendor that charges per message. How do you structure the decision?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists pros and cons (&quot;build gives control, buy is faster&quot;) with no criteria and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the criteria (cost over time, time to market, strategic importance, risk), compares costs at the expected volume, and recommends with a condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Finds the break-even volume, tests the decision against volume uncertainty and reversibility, counts the engineers' time, and proposes a staged path (buy now, build at a trigger).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would structure it on four criteria: cost over three years, time to market, how strategic the capability is, and risk and reversibility. Cost first, with invented inputs. The vendor charges $0.01 a message. Building costs $2,000,000 once, four engineers to maintain it at $200,000 each ($800,000 a year), and infrastructure of $0.001 a message. Over 36 months, buying costs 0.01 × messages a month × 36 and building costs $2,000,000 + 3 × $800,000 + 0.001 × messages a month × 36. At 8 million messages a month (today, invented) buying costs $2,880,000 and building $4,688,000, so buying is cheaper by $1,808,000. At 14 million the two are about equal ($5,040,000 against $4,904,000). At 30 million building is cheaper by $5,320,000. The break-even is $4,400,000 ÷ (($0.01 − $0.001) × 36) = about 13.6 million messages a month. The other criteria. Time: the vendor is live in about four weeks and a build takes about nine months (invented), which matters if messaging gates a launch. Strategic importance: if messaging is not what customers choose us for, building it is a distraction. Reversibility: buying is reversible if the product talks to the vendor through our own interface; building is not. My recommendation: buy now, behind an interface, because today's volume is below break-even and speed matters. Set a trigger to revisit: sustained volume above about 14 million a month for two quarters, or messaging becoming a differentiator, or vendor terms or reliability failing. I would change my mind if volume were already near 30 million or if the vendor could not meet a requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Leadership sets a goal to double weekly active users in a year. How do you structure the problem of how to get there?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists growth ideas (more marketing, new features) with no structure and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes weekly actives into additions and losses, checks what the goal requires, and ranks levers by size and cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the steady-state relationship between monthly additions and the loss rate to show what doubling requires, tests whether one lever can do it, and challenges the definition and the purpose of the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would start by writing weekly actives as a stock with flows. Suppose today there are 1,000,000 weekly actives (invented). Each month about 80,000 users are added (new and returning) and about 6% of the base, 60,000, drops out. Net growth is +20,000 a month. A base like this drifts toward a steady state of additions divided by the loss rate: 80,000 ÷ 0.06 = 1,333,333. After 12 months the base would be about 1,174,693, roughly 17% up, not double. What doubling requires. To reach 2,000,000 in 12 months with the same 6% loss rate, additions must be about 174,486 a month, 2.2 times today's. Cutting the loss rate from 6% to 4% with additions unchanged gets to only 1,387,290. Cutting the loss rate to 4% and raising additions to 150,000 gets to 2,065,048. So doubling needs both levers at once, with additions up by nearly two thirds more than today's and the loss rate down by a third (the combination of 150,000 additions and 4% loss, the numbers invented). Then the questions about the goal. What counts as active (one visit or one core action)? A looser definition could double the number without doubling the value. What is the number for: revenue, a market position, a story for investors? That decides whether retention (cheaper, compounds) or acquisition (faster, costly) should lead. So I would propose intermediate targets by lever, for example first-month loss, additions by channel and returning users, test the cheapest lever first, and report each quarter against the path the arithmetic implies. If leadership keeps the target, I would show them plainly that it needs about 1.9 times today's additions and a third lower loss rate together.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c03-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C03-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> structuring · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C03 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Trust and safety wants an ID check at sign-up to stop fraud. Growth says the check will cost sign-ups. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Picks a side, or says &quot;find a balance&quot;, with no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Puts both costs in one unit, estimates each from stated assumptions, and compares at least two options (check everyone, check no one) before deciding.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Finds the option between the extremes (a risk-based check), computes its value, names the assumption that decides it, proposes a test with guardrails, and says who decides and when to revisit.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would put both sides in one unit, dollars a month, with invented inputs. Suppose 100,000 sign-ups a month, 3% of them fraudulent (3,000), each fraudulent account costing $60, and each legitimate sign-up worth $25 of contribution. The check stops 90% of fraud and makes 15% of legitimate users give up. Check everyone: fraud stopped is 2,700 × $60 = $162,000; legitimate users lost are 14,550 × $25 = $363,750. Net: −$201,750 a month. Check no one: nothing gained or lost against today. So a blanket check loses money on these numbers, and the answer to &quot;safety or growth&quot; is neither extreme. Between them: a risk-based check. Suppose a risk score sends the riskiest 20,000 sign-ups (20%) to the check, and that group holds 70% of the fraud: 2,100 fraudulent and 17,900 legitimate. The check stops 1,890 fraudulent accounts, worth $113,400, and costs 2,685 legitimate users, worth $67,125. Net: +$46,275 a month. The assumption that decides it is how many legitimate users in the risky group give up. At 30% instead of 15%, the net becomes −$20,850. So I would run it as a staged test with a holdout, with guardrails on legitimate sign-up completion, support contacts and fraud losses, and expand only if the net is positive with its interval. Product, trust and safety and growth decide together on that rule, and I would review it monthly, because fraudsters adapt. Two things sit outside the arithmetic: harm to people that a dollar figure understates, and legal or partner requirements, which can make the check mandatory in some markets.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the number of coffee shops in a city of two million people.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Names a number (&quot;maybe a thousand&quot;) with no path to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a coffee shop, decomposes by demand and shop throughput, cross-checks, and gives a rounded answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States a range, identifies the dominant assumption, cross-checks per capita, and says what would change the estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll define a coffee shop as a place whose main business is selling coffee for takeaway or sit-down, excluding restaurants and hotel lounges. The answer is a stock: shops that exist today. I'll go from demand. Two million people. Suppose about 25% buy coffee out on a typical weekday, which is 500,000 purchases, assuming one purchase each. That is a judgment, anchored to my own week: I buy out about a third of days, and many people buy less. A shop serves about 400 customers a day: over a 12-hour day that is about one customer every 1.8 minutes, which feels right for a busy small shop and generous for a quiet one. Shops: 500,000 ÷ 400 = 1,250. Check. Per capita that is one shop per 1,600 people. In a coffee-heavy city that is plausible, and in a city with little coffee culture I would expect fewer. Second route, supply side: I'd guess a typical neighborhood of 10,000 people has five to eight coffee shops, which is one per 1,250 to 2,000 people, consistent with 1,250 shops for two million people. Range. The share of people who buy out dominates: at 15% I get about 750 shops, and at 35% about 1,750. Widening for shop throughput (300 to 500 customers a day) gives roughly 600 to 2,300. I'd say about 1,250, plausibly 800 to 2,500. If this were for a store-opening decision, I'd check how many shops already serve the target neighborhood and what share of people there buy coffee out.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many video calls happen each day on a work-chat app at a company of 10,000 employees?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Everyone has a few calls, so maybe 30,000,&quot; without separating calls from participants.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates calls from participations, decomposes, and checks plausibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Resolves the definition of a call, gives a range, notes that the share using video is the dominant assumption, and ties the result to a capacity or cost question.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> First the definition. A call is one meeting with several participants, so I need to separate participations (one person joining one call) from calls. I'll estimate participations and then divide by the average call size. The app is the company's work-chat app, and I'll count only calls on it, not calls on other tools. Ten thousand employees. Suppose 60% join at least one video call on a typical workday (the rest are in person, on other tools, or not at a desk): 6,000 people. Each of them joins about 2.5 calls: 15,000 participations. Average call size is about four people. So calls are 15,000 ÷ 4 = 3,750 per day. Check. Per employee that is 1.5 call participations a day for the whole company, which fits a knowledge-work company; it would be high for a manufacturing one. And 3,750 calls over about eight working hours is about 470 an hour, and the number running at the same moment is perhaps 100 to 300 at the busiest time, which is a plausible load for a company of this size. Range. The share who use video dominates, at 40% to 80%, which gives about 2,500 to 5,000 calls. Calls per user is next. I'd say about 3,750 calls a day, plausibly 2,500 to 5,000. If the question is about infrastructure, I'd next estimate the peak concurrent calls, because capacity is set by the peak, not the average.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate how many wireless earbuds are in active use in one large city.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Maybe a couple of million,&quot; with no structure.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes by population, age, phone ownership and earbud ownership, adds an active-use factor, and cross-checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes people from pairs, converts the stock to an annual sales flow through a replacement cycle as the second route, and explains how to add a brand-share factor if asked about one maker.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used; all figures are assumptions.</em> I'll define the quantity as pairs of wireless earbuds, of any maker, used at least weekly. That is a stock. I'll take a city of 10 million people. Decompose by people. Of 10 million, suppose 65% are of an age to own and use them (roughly 15 to 64): 6.5 million. About 85% of those have a smartphone, which I treat as a prerequisite: 5.5 million. About 40% of smartphone owners own wireless earbuds (a judgment, anchored to the proportion of my own acquaintances): 2.2 million owners. About 75% of owners use them at least weekly: 1.66 million people. Some people keep a second pair, so I'll take 1.1 pairs per active person: about 1.8 million pairs in active use. Check, using the flow. If a pair lasts about 2.5 years before replacement, a stock of 1.8 million implies about 730,000 pairs bought a year in the city, which is 7.3 per 100 people per year. That is high but plausible for a wealthy, phone-heavy city, and I'd expect it to be lower in a lower-income one. This second route does not use the ownership factor directly, so it is a real check on the plausibility of the stock. Sensitivity. Ownership share dominates (at 25% I get about 1.1 million pairs, at 55% about 2.5 million). If asked about one maker's earbuds, I would add a brand-share factor, say 30% to 60%, and I'd flag it as my weakest anchor, since it is the one I know least about. My answer: about 1.8 million pairs, plausibly 1.1 to 2.5 million. I would change it if I learned the city's smartphone penetration was well off my 85%. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that product and technical deep dives at Apple may include device-related estimates <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data is used or implied.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the annual revenue opportunity for a mobile wallet in a country of 50 million adults.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fifty million adults times something,&quot; with no funnel.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Builds a funnel from adults to users, then multiplies by transactions and a take rate, and states the assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the long-run potential from a near-term adoption path, gives a range driven by the take rate, and names the decision the number serves.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll define the opportunity as net revenue a wallet could earn from payments, per year, and I'll separate long-run potential from a year-three figure. Funnel. Fifty million adults. About 70% have a smartphone: 35 million. About 60% of those hold a bank account or equivalent that a wallet can connect to: 21 million. In the long run, perhaps 40% of them use a wallet regularly: 8.4 million users. Revenue per user. Suppose a regular user makes about 12 payments a month, 144 a year, at an average of $15, so $2,160 of annual payment value. If the wallet earns a take rate of 0.5%, revenue is about $10.80 per user per year. Total: 8.4 million × $10.80 ≈ $91 million a year. Near term. If adoption reaches 15% in year three: 21 million × 15% = 3.15 million users × $10.80 ≈ $34 million. Range. The take rate drives the spread: at 0.3% it is about $54 million a year at 40% adoption, and at 0.8% it is about $145 million. Adoption drives the timing more than the total. Check. Per user, $10.80 a year is small relative to the cost of acquiring a user, so the question is whether the wallet can earn other revenue (lending, merchant services) or must be very cheap to run. The estimate says the payments take rate alone is a thin business, which is the important finding. The decision it serves is whether to enter on payments alone, and I'd say not without a second revenue stream. Payment-method context is region-specific <code>[as of 2026-10; varies by market]</code>; in a country with an established low-cost interbank payment system, take rates may be lower than 0.5%, which would weaken the case further.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A retailer wants to deliver 200,000 same-day orders in one metro area. Estimate how many delivery vans it needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;200,000 divided by some number per van,&quot; with no consideration of stops, utilization or peak.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Converts orders to stops, uses stops per van per day, adds a utilization allowance, and states the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies stops per van as the dominant assumption (density), checks the cost per order, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no real operating data is used or implied; every figure is an assumption.</em> I'll define the target as vans in service on a peak day to complete 200,000 orders. Orders to stops. Some orders go to the same address, so suppose 1.2 orders per stop: 200,000 ÷ 1.2 ≈ 167,000 stops. A van's capacity is set by stops per day, which depends on how dense deliveries are. In a dense metro, perhaps 120 stops a day (about 15 an hour over an 8-hour route, including driving and handover). Vans: 167,000 ÷ 120 ≈ 1,390. No operation runs vehicles at 100% utilization because of breakdowns, imbalance between routes and time windows, so at 80% the fleet is about 1,390 ÷ 0.8 ≈ 1,740. Check. Cost: if a van and driver cost about $200 a day all-in, that is about $350,000 a day, or about $1.74 an order. That is plausible for dense delivery and would be too low for sparse areas. Per capita: if the metro has 10 million people, 200,000 orders is 2 per 100 people per day, which is high but not implausible for a mature same-day service. Sensitivity. Stops per van dominates: at 80 stops a day the fleet is about 2,600, and at 150 it is about 1,390. Orders per stop matters less. So I would ask about route density, and I'd compare two cities before committing. Decision. If the unit cost per order from vehicles alone is already $1.74, the retailer needs the order value or delivery fee to cover it, so I would also ask for average order value. I would change the plan if density were much lower than assumed, which would favor a different model (pickup points, lockers, or scheduled delivery days). <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that capacity and logistics estimates appear in some retail loops <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the network capacity needed for a live stream with five million concurrent viewers.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A lot of bandwidth, maybe a few hundred gigabits,&quot; with no calculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Multiplies viewers by bitrate, converts units correctly, adds headroom, and gives a server count.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles bits versus bytes, peak and ramp-up, total data volume and cost, and ties the numbers to a design decision such as a content network and bitrate tiers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll define the quantity as peak outbound bandwidth to viewers and the total data delivered for a two-hour event. Bandwidth. Five million concurrent viewers. With adaptive quality, many viewers receive a mid-tier stream, so I'll assume an average of 4 megabits per second. 5,000,000 × 4 Mbps = 20,000,000 Mbps = 20 terabits per second. In bytes, dividing by 8, that is 2.5 terabytes per second. I'm watching the units here: network capacity is in bits, storage and volume are in bytes. Volume. Two hours is 7,200 seconds. 2.5 TB/s × 7,200 = 18,000 TB = 18 petabytes. At an assumed delivery price of $0.01 per gigabyte (a judgment, and the kind of figure that changes with contracts <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>), 18 million gigabytes costs about $180,000. Capacity. If one edge server can send 40 gigabits per second, 20,000 Gbps ÷ 40 = 500 servers at full load. No one runs at 100%, so with a target of 50% utilization for headroom the fleet is about 1,000 servers, spread across regions because viewers are everywhere and a single site cannot carry the load. Check. Per viewer, 4 Mbps for two hours is 4 × 7,200 ÷ 8 = 3,600 megabytes, or 3.6 GB, and 5 million viewers × 3.6 GB = 18,000,000 GB, which matches the 18 petabytes. Both ways agree. Sensitivity and design. Bitrate dominates, so a lower top tier or a smarter ladder of quality levels cuts capacity directly: dropping the average from 4 to 3 Mbps saves 25%. The shape of the audience matters as much as the size: if viewers join within five minutes of the start, the peak is a ramp, and the system must be ready at the start, not on average. Because the ramp and the failure cost are what hurt, I'd ask about the distribution of start times and whether the stream can degrade gracefully (lower quality) rather than fail (C22 develops system-design answers).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your CEO says the market for our product is $10 billion. How would you test that in 15 minutes?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd research the market online and see if it's right.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the decomposition, rebuilds it bottom-up, finds the factor that differs, and gives a revised number.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates total from serviceable from obtainable, audits the claim for typical errors, gives a range, and says what to do with the gap.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd first ask where the number comes from, because a market claim is a decomposition in disguise. Suppose the claim is 5 million businesses times $2,000 a year, which is $10 billion. That is a clear structure, so I can test each factor. Factor one: 5 million businesses. Do all of them have the problem the product solves? I'd guess only about a fifth do, so 1 million. Factor two: can we reach them? A product sold through a certain channel reaches a share of them, say 60%, so 600,000. Factor three: the price. Is $2,000 what customers pay, or a list price for the highest tier? If typical buyers pay about $1,200, the serviceable market is 600,000 × $1,200 = $720 million. So the claim is about 14 times higher than my rebuild ($10 billion ÷ $0.72 billion). The gap comes from counting every business, counting them all as reachable and pricing them at the top tier. These are the usual errors: a total market presented as a serviceable one, and a price applied to buyers who would not pay it. Range. Taking each factor's plausible range (needing the product 10% to 30%, reachable 40% to 80%, price $800 to $1,800), the serviceable market is about 5 million × 0.10 × 0.40 × $800 = $160 million to 5 million × 0.30 × 0.80 × $1,800 = $2.16 billion, with a base of about $720 million. Even the high end is a fifth of the claim. What I'd do. I'd keep $10 billion as an upper bound on the total opportunity and use about $0.7 billion for planning, and I'd say which factor I'd test first: the share of businesses with the need, with ten customer interviews. I'd want to know what decision the number is being used for, because a $10 billion claim might be fine for a story to investors and wrong for a hiring plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many customer-support agents does a company need for a product with two million monthly users?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gives a number from instinct or a ratio of agents to users, with no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes into contacts, handling time and agent hours, states the assumptions, gives a range and a check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Chooses the swing assumption, converts the answer into cost per user, and says how deflection or peaks would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would build it from contacts and agent time. Two million monthly users. Suppose 8% contact support in a month (a judgment, anchored to products with good self-service): 160,000 contacts. Each takes about 12 minutes including notes: 160,000 × 12 ÷ 60 = 32,000 hours. An agent is paid for about 150 hours a month, but breaks, training and leave leave about 70% as handling time, so 105 hours: 32,000 ÷ 105 = about 305, so about 300 agents. Check. Two million users per 300 agents is one agent per 6,700 users, plausible for a product with good self-service and too lean for one with complex accounts. Cost: at $4,000 a month per agent, 300 agents cost $1.2 million, or $0.60 per user per month. Range. A contact rate of 5% gives 190 agents and 12% gives 457. So about 300, plausibly 190 to 460, and the contact rate is the swing assumption; I would take it from actual ticket counts. If the question is for a staffing plan, I would add that demand is not flat across the week.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many public electric-vehicle charging points does a city of three million people need in five years?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Names a number or a ratio with no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Estimates the stock of vehicles, the share that is electric and needs public charging, converts sessions into chargers, and gives a range.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds peak demand, identifies the swing assumption, and compares the result with a city further along instead of trusting it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would estimate a stock of vehicles first, then convert it into demand for chargers. Three million people. Suppose one car per four people: 750,000 cars. In five years suppose 10% are electric (a judgment; plausible range 5% to 20%): 75,000 vehicles. Suppose 70% can charge at home or work, so 30% rely on public charging: 22,500 vehicles. Each needs about two public sessions a month: 45,000 sessions a month, 1,500 a day. From sessions to chargers. A public charger handles about 8 sessions a day (about an hour each over eight busy hours, an assumption): 1,500 ÷ 8 = about 188. Demand clusters in evenings and at weekends, so I would plan for 1.5 times that: about 281 chargers. Check. 281 chargers for 75,000 electric vehicles is one per 267 vehicles. I would compare that with a city further along in adoption instead of trusting it. Range: electric share of 5% gives about 141 and 20% about 562, so about 281, plausibly 141 to 562. The electric share dominates, then the share who can charge at home. If this were for a procurement decision, I would stage the build against the adoption actually observed each year.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A designer says a change will save each user two seconds on a task they do many times a day. Is it worth building?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Says two seconds is too small to matter, or that every improvement is worth it, without numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Multiplies out the time saved, converts it to value, haircuts it for how time savings really behave, and compares with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the customer's value from what the company can capture, says what else the change might move (friction, retention), and proposes a cheap way to test it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would turn two seconds into a total and then into a decision. Suppose a work tool with 200,000 daily active seats, each doing the task 25 times a day (invented). Time saved: 200,000 × 25 × 2 seconds = 10,000,000 seconds, about 2,778 hours a day, and over 250 working days about 694,444 hours a year. Value. At a loaded cost of $40 an hour that is about $27.8 million a year of customer time. But two-second savings are not recovered as work; people do not convert slivers into output. Suppose only 30% is realized: about $8.3 million. The company captures a small part through retention and willingness to pay; suppose 5%: about $416,667 a year. Cost. Suppose two engineers for three months plus design and testing: about $150,000. Payback is about 4.3 months on the captured value. So it clears the bar, but the number rests on two weak assumptions, the realized share and the captured share. What I would add. The larger effect of a faster task may be less friction, which shows up as more use or fewer abandoned tasks, not as saved time. I would test a faster version on a subset of seats and read task completion and weekly use, instead of arguing about the value of two seconds. I would change my mind if the task is rare for most users, because then the total shrinks.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c04-11\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C04-11</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> estimation · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C04 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a country with 800 million adults, estimate the payments per second a payments app must handle at its evening peak.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gives an average rate, or a number with no peak and no decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes users, payments per user and the daily total, converts to a rate, and applies a peak factor with a stated reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the average from the peak minute, checks the ratio between them, gives a range, and says what the number means for how the system is sized.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would define the unit first: a completed payment. Adults: 800 million (invented). Suppose 60% have a bank or wallet account: 480 million. On a typical day suppose 25% of them pay with the app: 120 million active users. Each makes about 2.5 payments: 300 million payments a day, which is 3,472 a second on average. Payments are not spread evenly. Suppose the busiest hour carries 15% of the day: 45 million an hour, 12,500 a second. The peak minute runs above the peak hour, say 1.5 times: about 18,750 a second. Check. The peak is about 5.4 times the average, plausible because payments cluster in a few evening hours and around paydays. A ratio near 1 would mean flat demand and one near 20 would suggest a spike-driven product such as ticketing. Per capita, 120 million daily users is 15% of all adults, which I would test against any published usage figure. Range. The share of account holders who pay on a given day dominates: 15% to 35% gives about 11,250 to 26,250 a second at the peak. So about 18,750, plausibly 11,000 to 26,000. What it means. The system must be sized for the peak minute, with headroom for festival or payday days, not for the average of 3,472; so the question to engineering is the capacity at the busiest minute and what degrades first when it is exceeded.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What would you use as the success metric for a &quot;save for later&quot; feature in a news app?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of articles saved,&quot; with no link to value.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, chooses a reading-based primary metric with a definition, and adds a guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why saves alone are gameable, defines the denominator, adds a counter metric for unread clutter, and says how to learn whether the feature causes more reading.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The goal of save-for-later is that people read things they cared about but had no time for. The behavior that shows value is reading saved articles, not saving them, because saving costs a tap and does not mean anything was gained. So my primary metric is the saved-article read rate: of articles saved in a week, the share opened and read to at least 50% within seven days of saving. The numerator is saved articles read to 50% within seven days. The denominator is articles saved that week, excluding saves made by import or by a bulk tool. The unit is the saved article, and I'd report it by user as well, since a few heavy savers can dominate. Inputs: the share of weekly active readers who save at least one article, saves per saver, and the share of saved articles that get a reminder or reappear in a list. Guardrails: total reading time must not fall, which would suggest saving replaces reading now with reading never; and article load time must stay flat. My counter metric is the share of saved articles never opened after 30 days. If it climbs, the feature is a graveyard. Target: a read rate of 35% within eight weeks (assumed). To learn whether the feature causes more reading, I'd compare readers who see the save button with a holdout who do not, looking at articles read per week. I'd change my mind if saves rise and reads do not: the metric would show the feature is a habit of hoarding, and the fix would be reminders or surfacing saves at a better time.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the difference between a leading and a lagging indicator? Give a product example and a way it can go wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Defines both loosely without a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear definitions, an example pair, and why a team needs both.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the need to validate the leading indicator's link to the outcome, gives a failure mode (a leading indicator that stops predicting, or is gamed), and says how to check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A lagging indicator reports a result after it has happened: quarterly churn, annual revenue, 90-day retention. It is reliable and slow, so by the time it moves it is hard to change. A leading indicator moves earlier and predicts the result: how many days a new user was active in the first week. A product pair: 90-day retention is the lagging indicator for a learning app, and the share of new users who complete three lessons in their first week is the leading indicator. The team can see the leading indicator in seven days and act on it, while the lagging one takes three months. A team needs both: the lagging indicator says whether we succeeded and the leading one says whether we are on track in time to change course. How it goes wrong. First, the link may not be real. If users who complete three lessons are simply the ones who would have stayed anyway, pushing more users to do it may not raise retention. I'd check by correlation over past cohorts and then by an experiment that increases first-week lessons and watches 90-day retention. Second, the leading indicator becomes a target and is gamed: making the first three lessons very short raises the share completing them and teaches nothing. A counter metric such as a short quiz on what was taught would show it. Third, the relationship drifts as the product or audience changes, so I'd recheck it each quarter.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Define success metrics for a smartwatch sleep-tracking feature, including a privacy guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of people who use it and their sleep scores,&quot; with no guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets a goal, a primary usage metric, inputs, product guardrails (battery, accuracy) and a privacy guardrail.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats consent and on-device processing as part of the metric design, accounts for the bias from users who opt out of measurement, adds a counter metric for harm (anxiety about sleep scores), and avoids health claims it cannot support.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used or implied.</em> The goal is that people understand their sleep and can choose to act on it, which requires that they wear the watch to bed regularly and look at the results. The feature is not a medical product in this answer, so I'd measure usage and perceived usefulness, not health outcomes I cannot validate. Primary metric: sustained overnight use, defined as the share of eligible watch owners who wear the watch to sleep on at least four nights a week for four consecutive weeks. Numerator: owners meeting that rule in a four-week window. Denominator: owners with the feature available and the sleep setting turned on at the start of the window. Inputs: setup completion, nights worn per week, and morning review (the share of worn nights followed by opening the summary). Product guardrails: battery life through a full day after a night of tracking must not fall below the current standard, since people who cannot charge in the morning will drop the feature, and the rate of nights with no data due to wear or charging problems should not rise. Counter metrics: the share of users who turn tracking off within 30 days (to see if it annoys), and the rate of reports or survey responses from people who say that sleep scores make them anxious about sleep, because a score can create the problem it measures. Privacy guardrail: sleep data is sensitive personal data. Where possible I'd compute usage metrics on the device or in aggregate, report only counts and rates, and collect nothing at the individual level without consent. The guardrail metrics are the opt-in rate for sharing analytics and a count of data-access incidents, which must be zero. The cost is measurement bias: the data only covers people who opted in, who may be more engaged than those who did not. I'd say that in the result, and I'd supplement with an in-product survey that does not depend on tracking, to see whether opt-in users differ. I'd change my plan if the nights-worn distribution shows a bimodal pattern: some people wear it nightly and many never, which would point to setup or comfort and not to the feature itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you measure the success of a personalized recommendations row on a streaming service?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Click-through rate and hours watched.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines satisfying viewing as the goal, uses a funnel from impression to sustained viewing, and adds guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Warns that click and hours are gameable, adds a regret counter metric, considers diversity and long-term retention, and proposes a long-run holdout.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data or internal metrics are claimed.</em> The goal of the row is to help a member find something they want to watch quickly and enjoy it, so that they keep subscribing. A click is not the goal: a misleading thumbnail raises clicks and lowers satisfaction. The funnel: impression of the row, play started from the row, and sustained viewing (watching at least 10 minutes, or finishing the title). Primary metric: satisfied plays per active member per week, defined as plays started from the row that reach 10 minutes, divided by active members in the week, excluding autoplay previews. The 10-minute threshold filters accidental starts, and it can be tuned by title length. Inputs: the row's play rate (plays per impression), the sustained-view rate (plays reaching 10 minutes), and time from opening the service to a satisfied play. Guardrails: total viewing per member should not fall (which would mean the row replaces better choices), and the diversity of titles played (share of plays outside the most popular ten percent of titles) should not collapse, so the row does not narrow taste. Counter metric: regret, defined as plays abandoned within two minutes, which a clickbait-style row would raise. The long-run measure is retention. Satisfied plays may lead retention by weeks or months, so I'd run a long-term holdout of members who see a simpler row, and compare their cancellation rate. Short tests would see plays, and only a holdout would see whether the row helps keep members. I'd change my view if satisfied plays rose and retention did not, which would mean the row shifts viewing within the service but does not add value.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What metrics would you use to evaluate an AI writing assistant that suggests text as people type?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of users and number of suggestions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses acceptance and edit behavior as the primary signal, adds quality, latency and cost guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes that acceptance can mean trust or inattention, adds an audit of accepted suggestions, links cost to acceptance, and separates offline quality evaluation from online metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The goal is that people finish writing faster or better with suggestions they trust. The primary signal is suggestions accepted and kept: of suggestions shown, the share accepted and not substantially edited or deleted within a minute (the numerator is accepted suggestions that survive; the denominator is suggestions shown; the unit is the suggestion, reported also per user). Inputs: how often suggestions are shown relative to typing, acceptance rate, edit distance after acceptance, and the share of documents completed with the assistant on. Guardrails: latency of suggestions (the 95th percentile must be short enough to arrive before the user types past it), harmful or inappropriate suggestion rate, and cost per suggestion. A counter metric for over-acceptance: acceptance can rise when users stop reading carefully, so I'd audit a sample of accepted suggestions for errors and track the rate of later corrections. Cost links to the metric: if each suggestion costs about $0.004 and the acceptance rate is 25%, the cost per accepted suggestion is $0.004 ÷ 0.25 = $0.016. Raising the acceptance rate to 40% lowers it to $0.01, so quality improvements also lower the cost per accepted suggestion. Online metrics sit on top of an offline evaluation: before launch I'd score suggestions against a held-out set with human review, so I know quality before users see it (evals are covered in C24 and C25). I'd change my plan if acceptance is high but the audit finds frequent errors: the assistant would be trusted more than it deserves.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-06</h3>\n";
